        {
          "id": "34d7aa0c-9ce0-3fda-925f-e8cb6f590acf",
          "content": {
            "id": "34d7aa0c-9ce0-3fda-925f-e8cb6f590acf",
            "contentType": "VIDEO",
            "title": "CrowdStrike founder Kurtz buys into Mercedes F1 team",
            "description": "<title><body><p>STORY: CrowdStrike founder and chief executive George Kurtz has bought into the Mercedes Formula One team.</p><p>Kurtz is taking\u00a015% of the one-third stake held by principal Toto Wolff, both parties announced on Thursday.</p><p>No financial details were given for the personal acquisition but media reports last week said the deal valued the former champions at a record $6 billion.</p><p>Wolff, Mercedes-Benz and Jim Ratcliffe's petrochemicals giant Ineos each hold one-third stakes and the governance and leadership of the team will not be affected by the deal.</p><p>Kurtz will become the team's technology adviser and joins the strategic steering committee alongside Wolff, Ratcliffe and\u00a0Mercedes-Benz chairman Ola Kaellenius.</p><p>In an interview with reporters, Kurtz said he sees a great opportunity to grow the sport, particularly in the U.S. market.</p><p>He said he had total alignment with Wolff, subscribed to his leadership style and had been discussing taking a stake for several years.</p><p>He ruled out one day taking on a greater role in the team, saying his focus remains with Cybersecurity company\u00a0CrowdStrike.</p><p>CrowdStrike has been a team partner since 2019 with branding on the car and apparel.</p><p>Mercedes won eight constructors' titles in a row from 2014 to 2021, and are currently second overall behind champions McLaren, who also use the German manufacturer's engines.</p></body></title>",
            "summary": "<body><p>STORY: CrowdStrike founder and chief executive George Kurtz has bought into the Mercedes Formula One team.</p><p>Kurtz is taking&nbsp;15% of the one-third stake held by principal Toto Wolff, both parties announced on Thursday.</p><p>No financial details were given for the personal acquisition but media reports last week said the deal valued the former champions at a record $6 billion.</p><p>Wolff, Mercedes-Benz and Jim Ratcliffe's petrochemicals giant Ineos each hold one-third stakes and the governance and leadership of the team will not be affected by the deal.</p><p>Kurtz will become the team's technology adviser and joins the strategic steering committee alongside Wolff, Ratcliffe and&nbsp;Mercedes-Benz chairman Ola Kaellenius.</p><p>In an interview with reporters, Kurtz said he sees a great opportunity to grow the sport, particularly in the U.S. market.</p><p>He said he had total alignment with Wolff, subscribed to his leadership style and had been discussing taking a stake for several years.</p><p>He ruled out one day taking on a greater role in the team, saying his focus remains with Cybersecurity company&nbsp;CrowdStrike.</p><p>CrowdStrike has been a team partner since 2019 with branding on the car and apparel.</p><p>Mercedes won eight constructors' titles in a row from 2014 to 2021, and are currently second overall behind champions McLaren, who also use the German manufacturer's engines.</p></body>",
